@prefix : <https://example.org/eu-ai-act-compliance#> .
@prefix airo: <https://w3id.org/airo#> .
@prefix dpv: <https://w3id.org/dpv#> .
@prefix eu-aiact: <https://w3id.org/dpv/legal/eu/aiact#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

:AIRegulatorySandboxInstance a eu-aiact:AIRegulatorySandbox ;
    rdfs:label "Generic AI regulatory sandbox"@en ;
    :hasCondition :appropriate_data_transfer_safeguards_art60_4e,
        :cooperation_and_roles_in_testing_art60_4h,
        :effective_oversight_and_qualified_personnel_art60_4j,
        :informed_consent_or_law_enforcement_exception_art60_4i,
        :notification_of_suspension_termination_and_outcomes_art60_8,
        :protection_of_vulnerable_groups_art60_4g,
        :provider_establishment_or_representative_art60_4d,
        :registration_of_testing_art60_4c,
        :reversibility_of_ai_system_outputs_art60_4k,
        :testing_duration_limit_and_extension_art60_4f,
        :testing_plan_submission_and_approval_art60_4a_b ;
    :hasSummary "Article 58 specifies detailed arrangements for the establishment, development, implementation, operation, and supervision of AI regulatory sandboxes, including eligibility, procedures, participant terms, and cooperation among actors."^^xsd:string,
        "Article 59 establishes conditions under which personal data lawfully collected for other purposes may be processed in an AI regulatory sandbox to develop certain AI systems in the public interest, with specific monitoring, protection, and transparency obligations."^^xsd:string,
        "Member States shall ensure their competent authorities establish at least one AI regulatory sandbox at national level operational by 2 August 2026, fostering innovation, testing, and validation of AI systems before market placement."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_I a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex I — List of Union harmonisation legislation"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/1/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This Annex lists various European Union laws that aim to standardize rules across member states. These laws cover a wide range of areas, including machinery, toy safety, recreational watercraft, lifts, equipment for potentially explosive atmospheres, radio equipment, pressure equipment, cableway installations, personal protective equipment, appliances burning gaseous fuels, medical devices, civil aviation security, two- or three-wheel vehicles, agricultural and forestry vehicles, marine equipment, rail system, motor vehicles and their trailers, and aircrafts."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_II a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex II — List of criminal offences referred to in Article 5(1), first subparagraph, point (h)(iii)"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/2/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This Annex provides a list of criminal offences that are mentioned in Article 5(1). Using artificial intelligence (AI) for any of these purposes is prohibited by the EU AI Act."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_V a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex V — EU declaration of conformity"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/5/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "Providers of high-risk AI systems must create a written declaration of conformity for each system. This Annex details which elements must be included within the declaration of conformity. This includes the AI system's name and type, the provider's name and address, and a statement that the provider is solely responsible for the declaration. It must also confirm that the AI system complies with this regulation and any other relevant EU laws. If the AI system processes personal data, it must comply with a couple of other EU regulations. The declaration should also reference any relevant standards used, details of any conformity assessment, and the declaration's issue date and signatory."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_X a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex X — Union legislative acts on large-scale IT systems in the area of Freedom, Security and Justice"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/10/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "There are special rules for large-scale IT systems and high-risk AI systems that are already in operation – they will only be required to comply with the AI Act by 2030 according to Article 111. This Annex lists the various large-scale IT systems that these special rules apply to:The Schengen Information System, which helps manage border checks and return of illegal immigrants;The Visa Information System, which sets conditions for accessing other EU information systems;Eurodac, which compares biometric data to identify illegal immigrants;The Entry/Exit System, which records entry and exit data of non-EU nationals;The European Travel Information and Authorisation System;The European Criminal Records Information System;And regulations for interoperability between these systems."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_XI a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex XI — Technical documentation referred to in Article 53(1), point (a) — technical documentation for providers of general-purpose AI models"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/11/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "Article 53 requires providers of general-purpose AI models to provide detailed technical documentation. This Annex specifies what documentation is required, including a general description of the AI model, its intended tasks, acceptable use policies, release date, distribution methods, architecture, and license. It should also detail the model's design, training process, data used, and energy consumption. For AI models with systemic risk, additional information is required, including evaluation strategies, adversarial testing measures, and a description of the system architecture. This is to ensure transparency and accountability in the use of AI systems."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_XII a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex XII — Transparency information referred to in Article 53(1), point (b) — technical documentation for providers of general-purpose AI models to downstream providers that integrate the model into their AI system"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/12/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "Article 53 requires providers of general-purpose AI models to provide detailed information to those who integrate these models into their AI systems. This Annex specifies what information is required, including a general description of the AI model, its intended tasks, how it can be integrated into other systems, its release date and distribution methods, and how it interacts with other hardware or software. It also requires information on the model's architecture, input and output formats, and licensing. Additionally, providers must describe the model's development process, including the technical requirements for integration, data used for training and testing, and data sourcing methods."^^xsd:string .

:Annex_XIII a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex XIII — Criteria for the designation of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk referred to in Article 51"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/13/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "Article 51 explains how an AI model is classified as having systemic risk. When it is involved in making this determination, the European Commission will take into account the criteria listed in this Annex.Factors considered include: the model's complexity, the quality and size of the data used, the computational power needed to train the model, the type of input and output the model uses, the model's performance on various tasks, its level of independence and scalability, and its impact on the market. If the AI model is used by at least 10,000 registered businesses in the EU, it is assumed to have a high market impact."^^xsd:string .

:Article_1 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 1 — Subject matter"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_1_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_1_2_g ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/1/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This law aims to improve the European market by promoting the use of artificial intelligence (AI) that is safe, respects human rights, and protects health, safety, and the environment. It sets out rules for how AI can be sold, used, and monitored in the EU, and bans certain AI practices. It also sets specific rules for high-risk AI systems and their operators, and requires certain AI systems to be transparent. The law also includes rules for selling general-purpose AI models and measures to support innovation, particularly for small businesses and start-ups."^^xsd:string .

:Article_100 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 100 — Administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices and agencies"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_100_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_1_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_100_7 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/100/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Data Protection Supervisor can fine EU institutions if they break rules around artificial intelligence (AI). The size of the fine depends on factors like the seriousness and length of the rule-breaking, how many people were affected, and what the institution did to fix the problem. If an institution breaks the rules in Article 5, they can be fined up to €1.5 million. For other rule-breaking, the fine can be up to €750,000. The institution has the right to defend itself before the fine is decided. Money from fines goes into the EU's general budget. The Supervisor must report annually on the fines it has given out."^^xsd:string .

:Article_102 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 102 — Amendment to Regulation (EC) No 300/2008"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32008R0300 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_102 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/102/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about changes to a previous regulation (EC No 300/2008). It says that when the EU is creating detailed rules about the technical aspects and approval processes for security equipment that uses artificial intelligence, they need to consider the requirements listed in a specific section of another regulation (EU 2024/...)."^^xsd:string .

:Article_103 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 103 — Amendment to Regulation (EU) No 167/2013"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32013R0167 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_103 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/103/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about a change to a European Union regulation from 2013. The change is about how the EU makes rules related to artificial intelligence (AI) systems that are used for safety. When making these rules, the EU now has to consider certain requirements that are outlined in another section of the regulation."^^xsd:string .

:Article_104 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 104 — Amendment to Regulation (EU) No 168/2013"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32013R0168 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_104 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/104/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about a change to a previous regulation. It says that when new rules are made about artificial intelligence (AI) systems that are used for safety, they need to consider the requirements listed in a specific section of another regulation. This is to ensure that the AI systems are safe and meet the standards set by the European Parliament and Council."^^xsd:string .

:Article_105 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 105 — Amendment to Directive 2014/90/EU"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32014L0090 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_105 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/105/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about updating a previous law (Directive 2014/90/EU). It adds a new section stating that when the European Commission is working on activities related to artificial intelligence (AI) systems that are considered safety components, it must consider certain requirements. These requirements are found in Chapter III, Section 2, of a specific EU regulation. This includes when the Commission is adopting technical specifications and testing standards for these AI systems."^^xsd:string .

:Article_106 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 106 — Amendment to Directive (EU) 2016/797"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_106 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/106/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about a change to a European Union law from 2016. The change says that when making new rules related to artificial intelligence (AI) systems that are considered safety components, the requirements from a specific section of another EU regulation must be considered. This means that when the EU is creating new AI laws, they have to think about certain safety rules that already exist."^^xsd:string .

:Article_107 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 107 — Amendment to Regulation (EU) 2018/858"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32018R0858 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_107 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/107/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about a change to a European Union regulation from 2018. The change means that when new rules are made about artificial intelligence (AI) systems that are used for safety, they must consider certain requirements. These requirements are outlined in a specific section of another regulation. This ensures that safety is a priority when creating AI systems."^^xsd:string .

:Article_108 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 108 — Amendments to Regulation (EU) 2018/1139"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32018R1139 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_108,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_108_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/108/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU has made changes to its 2018 regulation on artificial intelligence (AI). These changes mean that when new rules are being made about AI systems that are used for safety, the requirements in a specific section of another regulation (Regulation (EU) 2024/…) must be considered. This applies to both implementing acts (rules that help enforce laws) and delegated acts (rules that are made by someone given authority by the original law-maker). The changes affect several articles (sections) of the 2018 regulation."^^xsd:string .

:Article_109 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 109 — Amendment to Regulation (EU) 2019/2144"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R2144 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_109 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/109/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about a change to a European Union regulation from 2019. It says that when new rules are being made about artificial intelligence systems that are used for safety, they need to consider the requirements listed in a specific section of another regulation. This is to ensure that these AI systems are safe and meet certain standards."^^xsd:string .

:Article_110 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 110 — Amendment to Directive (EU) 2020/1828"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_110 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/110/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Union has made an amendment to a directive from 2020. This change adds a new point to the directive, which introduces harmonised rules on artificial intelligence. These rules also make changes to several other regulations and directives, including the Artificial Intelligence Act. This is all part of the EU's ongoing efforts to regulate artificial intelligence and protect the interests of consumers."^^xsd:string .

:Article_2 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 2 — Scope"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32002L0058,
        :Legislation_32016R0679,
        :Legislation_32022R2065 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_2_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_10,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_11,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_12,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_1_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_2_9 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This EU regulation applies to anyone who makes, uses, imports, or distributes AI systems in the EU, regardless of where they are based. It also applies to AI systems used in the EU, even if they are made elsewhere. However, it does not apply to AI systems used for military, defense, or national security purposes, or to AI systems used by foreign public authorities or international organizations for law enforcement and judicial cooperation, provided they protect individuals' rights. The regulation also does not apply to AI systems used for scientific research and development, or to AI systems not yet on the market. It does not affect existing EU laws on data protection, privacy, and confidentiality. It also does not apply to individuals using AI systems for personal, non-professional activities, or to AI systems released under free and open-source licenses, unless they are high-risk or fall under certain articles."^^xsd:string .

:Article_28 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 28 — Notifying authorities"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32008R0765 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_28_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_28_7 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/28/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU AI Act states that each member country must have at least one authority responsible for assessing and monitoring AI conformity assessment bodies. These authorities must work together across all member states. Countries can choose to have this done by a national accreditation body. These authorities must be impartial and free from conflicts of interest. Decisions must be made by different people than those who did the assessments. These authorities can't offer the same services as the assessment bodies or provide consultancy services. They must keep information confidential and have enough skilled staff in areas like IT, AI, and law."^^xsd:string .

:Article_29 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 29 — Application of a conformity assessment body for notification"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_29_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_29_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_29_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_29_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/29/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains that organizations that assess AI systems for compliance with EU standards must apply to the relevant authority in the country where they are based. They must provide details about their assessment activities, the types of AI systems they can evaluate, and any relevant accreditation. If they don't have accreditation, they must provide other evidence to show they meet the necessary requirements. If they are already recognized under other EU laws, they can use those credentials to support their application. They must also keep their documentation up to date to show ongoing compliance."^^xsd:string .

:Article_32 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 32 — Presumption of conformity with requirements relating to notified bodies"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_32 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/32/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that if a body assessing AI systems can show it meets the criteria set out in the relevant EU standards, it will be assumed to also meet the requirements of Article 31. This is as long as the standards it follows cover the same requirements as Article 31. The references to these standards are published in the Official Journal of the European Union."^^xsd:string .

:Article_33 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 33 — Subsidiaries of notified bodies and subcontracting"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_33_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_33_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_33_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_33_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/33/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that if a notified body (an organization responsible for checking that products meet certain standards) outsources tasks or uses a subsidiary company, it must ensure that these parties meet the required standards and inform the relevant authority. The notified body is fully responsible for the work done by these parties. Such outsourcing or use of subsidiaries can only happen with the provider's agreement. The notified body must also make a list of their subsidiaries public. All documents related to the qualifications and work of these parties must be kept for five years after the subcontracting ends."^^xsd:string .

:Article_35 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 35 — Identification numbers and lists of notified bodies"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_35_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_35_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/35/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Commission will give each approved organization (notified body) a unique ID number, even if the organization is recognized under multiple EU laws. The Commission will also publish a list of these organizations, including their ID numbers and what they do. This list will be kept current."^^xsd:string .

:Article_36 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 36 — Changes to notifications"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_36_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_7_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_8_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_8_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_9,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_9_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_36_9_b ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/36/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains the procedures for changes to notifications about AI systems. If a body that assesses AI systems decides to stop its activities, it must inform the relevant authorities and providers. Its certificates may remain valid for nine months if another body takes over its responsibilities. If a body fails to meet requirements or obligations, the notifying authority can restrict, suspend or withdraw its designation. If a body's designation is suspended or withdrawn, it must inform the providers and the authority must ensure the body's files are kept and assess the impact on the certificates issued."^^xsd:string .

:Article_38 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 38 — Coordination of notified bodies"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_38_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_38_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_38_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/38/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that the Commission will ensure that organizations responsible for checking the safety of high-risk AI systems work together effectively. This will be done through a special group made up of these organizations. Each authority that notifies these organizations must ensure they participate in this group, either directly or through representatives. The Commission will also facilitate the sharing of knowledge and best practices among these authorities."^^xsd:string .

:Article_39 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 39 — Conformity assessment bodies of third countries"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_39 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/39/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This section says that organizations from countries outside the EU, which have an agreement with the EU, can be allowed to perform the duties of notified bodies under this regulation. However, they must either meet the requirements set out in Article 31 or ensure they follow rules that are equivalent. In simpler terms, these organizations can do the job, but they must follow the same or similar rules as EU organizations."^^xsd:string .

:Article_4 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 4 — AI literacy"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that companies that create and use AI systems must make sure their employees and anyone else who operates or uses these systems on their behalf are well-educated about AI. This includes considering their technical knowledge, experience, education, and training, as well as the context in which the AI systems will be used and the people or groups who will be using them."^^xsd:string .

:Article_40 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 40 — Harmonised standards and standardisation deliverables"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32012R1025 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_40_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_40_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_40_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/40/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that high-risk AI systems or general-purpose AI models that comply with certain standards will be considered in line with the requirements of this regulation. The Commission will issue standardisation requests to cover all requirements and obligations of this regulation. These requests will also ask for improvements in AI systems' resource performance, such as reducing energy consumption. The Commission will consult with the Board and relevant stakeholders when preparing these requests. The aim is to promote investment and innovation in AI, increase legal certainty, and enhance competitiveness and growth in the Union market."^^xsd:string .

:Article_41 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 41 — Common specifications"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32012R1025 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_41_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_iii,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_1_iv,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_41_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/41/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU Commission can create common rules for AI systems if certain conditions are met, such as if European standardisation organisations don't accept a request to create a standard, or if the standards created don't address fundamental rights concerns. If AI systems meet these common rules, they are assumed to meet the requirements of the law. If they don't, they must prove they meet the requirements in another way. If a member state thinks the common rules don't meet the requirements, they can ask the Commission to change them."^^xsd:string .

:Article_42 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 42 — Presumption of conformity with certain requirements"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R0881 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_42_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_42_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/42/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article basically says that high-risk AI systems that have been trained and tested on data specific to their intended use are assumed to meet certain requirements. Also, if these AI systems have been certified or have a statement of conformity under a cybersecurity scheme, they are assumed to meet the cybersecurity requirements, as long as the certificate or statement covers those requirements."^^xsd:string .

:Article_44 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 44 — Certificates"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_44_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_44_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_44_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/44/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that certificates for AI systems must be written in a language that can be easily understood by the relevant authorities in the country where the certificate is issued. These certificates are valid for up to five years for certain AI systems, and four years for others. They can be extended upon request, but only after a re-assessment. If an AI system no longer meets the necessary requirements, the certificate can be suspended or withdrawn, unless the provider takes corrective action. There must also be an appeal process for decisions made about these certificates."^^xsd:string .

:Article_45 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 45 — Information obligations of notified bodies"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_45_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_45_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/45/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that notified bodies (organizations that assess the conformity of certain products before they are sold in the EU) must inform the notifying authority about any certificates, approvals, or changes related to their assessments. They also need to share information about any requests they receive from market surveillance authorities. Additionally, they must inform other notified bodies about any approvals or certificates they have issued, refused, suspended, or withdrawn. They should also share information about negative and positive assessment results with similar bodies. However, they must keep all this information confidential."^^xsd:string .

:Article_52 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 52 — Procedure"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_52_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_52_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_52_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_52_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_52_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_52_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/52/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that if a general AI model meets certain conditions, the provider must inform the Commission within two weeks. The Commission can then decide if the AI model poses systemic risks. If the provider argues that the AI model doesn't pose such risks, the Commission can reject these arguments if they're not convincing. The Commission can also designate an AI model as risky based on certain criteria. If a provider disagrees with this designation, they can request a reassessment after six months. The Commission will maintain a list of risky AI models, while respecting intellectual property rights and business confidentiality."^^xsd:string .

:Article_57 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 57 — AI regulatory sandboxes"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_57_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_10,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_11,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_12,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_13,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_14,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_15,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_16,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_17,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_57_9_e ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/57/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU is requiring member states to create at least one \"AI regulatory sandbox\" at a national level. These sandboxes are controlled environments where AI systems can be developed, tested, and validated before being released to the market. The goal is to foster innovation while identifying and mitigating any risks, particularly those related to fundamental rights, health, and safety. The sandboxes will also provide guidance on regulatory expectations and requirements. If an AI system is successfully tested in a sandbox, the provider can use this as proof of compliance with regulations. The sandboxes are also intended to facilitate cross-border cooperation and share best practices."^^xsd:string .

:Article_58 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 58 — Detailed arrangements for, and functioning of, AI regulatory sandboxes"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_58_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_2_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_58_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/58/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU is setting up AI regulatory sandboxes to test and develop AI systems. These sandboxes will be open to any provider who meets the eligibility criteria. The process for applying, participating, and exiting the sandbox will be simple and clearly communicated. Access to the sandboxes will be free for small businesses and start-ups. The sandboxes will also help providers comply with regulations and encourage cooperation with other actors in the AI ecosystem. The duration of participation in the sandbox will depend on the complexity of the project. The sandboxes will also facilitate the development of tools for testing and assessing AI systems."^^xsd:string .

:Article_59 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 59 — Further processing of personal data for developing certain AI systems in the public interest in the AI regulatory sandbox"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32016R0679,
        :Legislation_32018R1725 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_59_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_iii,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_iv,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_1_v,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_59_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/59/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU AI Act allows for the use of personal data in a controlled environment, known as a regulatory sandbox, for the development, training, and testing of AI systems. This is only permitted if the AI system is being developed for the public interest, such as public safety, health, environmental protection, energy sustainability, transport safety, and public administration. The data must be necessary, risks must be monitored and mitigated, and the data must be kept separate and secure. The data cannot be shared outside the sandbox and must be deleted once the project is finished. The process and results must be documented and a summary published, unless it involves sensitive law enforcement data."^^xsd:string .

:Article_63 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 63 — Derogations for specific operators"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_63_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_63_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/63/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that small businesses (microenterprises) can follow a simplified version of the quality management system required by the Act, as long as they don't have partner or linked businesses. The Commission will create guidelines for this. However, this doesn't mean these businesses are exempt from other requirements or obligations in the Act, including those in Articles 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 72 and 73. This is to ensure that the level of protection and compliance with high-risk AI systems is not compromised."^^xsd:string .

:Article_64 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 64 — AI Office"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_64_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_64_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/64/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU is creating an \"AI Office\" to improve its knowledge and skills in artificial intelligence (AI). This office will be supported by the member countries of the EU, who will help it carry out its duties as outlined in the regulations."^^xsd:string .

:Article_65 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 65 — Establishment and structure of the European Artificial Intelligence Board"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_65_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_4_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_4_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_4_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_65_8 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/65/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Union is setting up a European Artificial Intelligence Board. Each EU member country will have a representative on the board, serving for three years. The board will also include observers from the European Data Protection Supervisor and the AI Office. The board can invite other experts to meetings as needed. The representatives will be responsible for coordinating AI regulation in their home countries and will adopt the board's rules by a two-thirds majority. The board will also have two sub-groups to discuss market surveillance and notifying authorities. The board will be chaired by a representative from one of the member states."^^xsd:string .

:Article_69 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 69 — Access to the pool of experts by the Member States"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_69_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_69_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_69_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/69/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that member countries can ask for help from a panel of experts to enforce the rules of the Act. These countries might have to pay for this advice, with the cost structure detailed in another part of the Act. The fees will consider the need for effective implementation, cost-effectiveness, and ensuring all countries can access the experts. The European Commission will help countries access these experts in a timely manner and ensure that the support provided is well-organized and valuable."^^xsd:string .

:Article_7 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 7 — Amendments to Annex III"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_7_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_2_k,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_3_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_7_3_b ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/7/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU Commission can change the list of high-risk AI systems if they meet certain conditions. These include being used in areas already listed as high-risk, and posing a health, safety, or rights risk equal to or greater than current high-risk systems. The Commission will consider factors like the AI's purpose, how much it's used, the data it processes, its autonomy, any previous harm caused, potential harm, and the ability to correct or reverse outcomes. The Commission can also remove systems from the list if they no longer pose significant risks and their removal doesn't decrease overall protection levels."^^xsd:string .

:Article_71 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 71 — EU database for high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_71_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_71_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_71_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_71_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_71_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_71_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/71/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Commission, in partnership with member states, will create and maintain a database of high-risk AI systems. This database will include information about both high-risk and non-high-risk AI systems. The data will be provided by the AI system provider or their representative, or by the public authority deploying the system. Most of the information in the database will be publicly available and user-friendly. However, some information will only be accessible to market surveillance authorities and the Commission, unless the provider consents to public access. The database will only contain personal data necessary for its operation. The Commission will manage the database and provide technical and administrative support."^^xsd:string .

:Article_74 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 74 — Market surveillance and control of AI systems in the Union market"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32013L0036,
        :Legislation_32013R1024,
        :Legislation_32016R0679,
        :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_74_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_10,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_11,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_12,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_13,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_13_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_13_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_14,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_74_9 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/74/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article outlines the rules for market surveillance and control of AI systems in the EU. It states that AI systems are subject to the same regulations as other economic products. Market surveillance authorities must report annually to the Commission and national competition authorities about any potential issues or prohibited practices. High-risk AI systems are subject to additional scrutiny, especially those used by financial institutions or for law enforcement purposes. The authorities can also propose joint activities to promote compliance and identify non-compliance. They can access the source code of high-risk AI systems under certain conditions."^^xsd:string .

:Article_81 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 81 — Union safeguard procedure"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32012R1025 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_81_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_81_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_81_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/81/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains a safeguard procedure. If a member state or the Commission objects to an AI-related measure taken by another member state, the Commission will review the measure. If the Commission finds the measure justified, all member states must take appropriate actions, such as removing the AI system from their market. If the measure is unjustified, the member state must withdraw it. If the AI system's non-compliance is due to shortcomings in standards or specifications, a specific procedure will be applied."^^xsd:string .

:Article_84 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 84 — Union AI testing support structures"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_84_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_84_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/84/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Commission will appoint one or more organizations to test and support AI systems in the EU, as outlined in a previous regulation. These organizations will also offer independent technical or scientific advice to the AI Board, the Commission, or market monitoring authorities when asked."^^xsd:string .

:Article_85 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 85 — Right to lodge a complaint with a market surveillance authority"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_85 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/85/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This part of the EU AI Act says that any individual or organization who believes that the rules of this Act have been broken can file a complaint with the appropriate market surveillance authority. This authority will then consider the complaint when they are monitoring the market. The way they handle these complaints will follow specific procedures that the authority has set up."^^xsd:string .

:Article_87 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 87 — Reporting of infringements and protection of reporting persons"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_87 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/87/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article is about reporting any violations of the AI Act and protecting those who report these violations. It refers to a previous EU directive from 2019, which sets out the rules for this. In simple terms, if someone breaks the rules of the AI Act, you can report it. And if you do report it, you will be protected under EU law."^^xsd:string .

:Article_88 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 88 — Enforcement of the obligations of providers of general-purpose AI models"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_88_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_88_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/88/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that the European Commission has the sole authority to oversee and enforce rules related to general-purpose AI models. The AI Office will carry out these tasks, but this doesn't affect the Commission's organizational powers or the division of responsibilities between EU countries. Additionally, market surveillance authorities can ask the Commission to use its powers to help them fulfill their duties under this regulation, if necessary and appropriate."^^xsd:string .

:Article_90 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 90 — Alerts of systemic risks by the scientific panel"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_90_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_3_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_3_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_90_3_c ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/90/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains that a scientific panel can raise an alert to the AI Office if they suspect a general-purpose AI model poses a significant risk at a Union level, or if it meets certain conditions. If such an alert is raised, the Commission can use its powers to assess the situation. The AI Office must inform the Board of any actions taken. The alert must be well-reasoned and include contact information of the AI model provider, a description of the relevant facts and reasons for the alert, and any other relevant information."^^xsd:string .

:Article_93 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 93 — Power to request measures"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_93_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_93_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_93_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_93_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_93_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_93_3 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/93/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that if necessary, the Commission can ask AI providers to take steps to meet certain obligations, implement measures to reduce risk, or limit the availability of their AI model. Before asking for these measures, the AI Office can start a discussion with the AI provider. If the provider agrees to take steps to reduce risk during this discussion, the Commission can make these commitments mandatory and declare that no further action is needed."^^xsd:string .

:Article_94 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 94 — Procedural rights of economic operators of the general-purpose AI model"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_94 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/94/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that the rules from Article 18 of Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 also apply to those who provide general-purpose AI models. This means they have certain rights when it comes to procedures. However, this doesn't affect any other specific procedural rights that are mentioned elsewhere in this regulation."^^xsd:string .

:Article_96 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 96 — Guidelines from the Commission on the implementation of this Regulation"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_96_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_1_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_96_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/96/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The European Commission will create guidelines on how to practically implement the new AI regulation. This includes how to apply the rules, what practices are prohibited, how to make changes, and how to be transparent. The guidelines will also explain how this regulation fits with other EU laws. The Commission will pay special attention to the needs of small businesses, local authorities, and sectors most affected by this regulation. The guidelines will be updated as needed, either at the request of member states, the AI Office, or the Commission itself."^^xsd:string .

:Article_98 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 98 — Committee procedure"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32011R0182 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_98_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_98_2 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/98/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This section of the EU AI Act states that the Commission will be supported by a committee. This committee is defined according to Regulation (EU) No 182/2011. If this paragraph is referred to, then Article 5 of Regulation (EU) No 182/2011 should be applied. In simpler terms, the Commission has a team to help it, and the rules for this team are set out in a specific EU regulation."^^xsd:string .

:EUAIAct2024 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        :AIActRegulation ;
    rdfs:label "Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — EU AI Act"@en ;
    :amends :Legislation_32008R0300,
        :Legislation_32013R0167,
        :Legislation_32013R0168,
        :Legislation_32014R0090,
