        :Paragraph_Article_3_49,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_50,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_51,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_52,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_53,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_54,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_55,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_56,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_57,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_58,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_59,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_60,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_61,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_62,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_63,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_64,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_65,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_66,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_67,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_68,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_9,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_3_iii ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article provides definitions for various terms related to artificial intelligence (AI) systems. An AI system is a machine-based system that can operate autonomously and adapt after deployment, generating outputs like predictions or decisions. A provider is a person or entity that develops an AI system and places it on the market. A deployer is a person or entity that uses an AI system. Other terms defined include importer, distributor, operator, and authorised representative. The article also defines terms related to data used in AI systems, such as training data, validation data, and testing data. It also covers terms related to the regulation and monitoring of AI systems, such as conformity assessment, market surveillance authority, and AI regulatory sandbox."^^xsd:string .

:Article_30 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 30 — Notification procedure"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_30_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_30_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_30_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_30_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_30_5 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/30/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains the process for notifying authorities about AI systems in the EU. Authorities can only notify assessment bodies that meet certain requirements. They must use a specific electronic tool to notify the Commission and other member states about each assessment body. The notification must include detailed information about the AI system and proof of the assessment body's competence. If no objections are raised within a certain time frame, the assessment body can start its activities. If there are objections, the Commission will consult with relevant parties and decide if the authorization is justified."^^xsd:string .

:Article_66 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 66 — Tasks of the Board"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_66,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_iii,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_iv,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_k,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_l,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_m,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_n,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_o,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_v,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_vi,
        :Paragraph_Article_66_vii ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/66/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The Board's role, as outlined in Article 66 of the EU AI Act, is to assist and advise the Commission and Member States in applying the regulation effectively and consistently. This includes coordinating with national authorities, sharing expertise and best practices, advising on the enforcement of rules on AI models, and harmonising administrative practices. The Board can also issue recommendations on relevant matters, support the Commission in promoting AI literacy, facilitate the development of common criteria, cooperate with other Union institutions, and assist in developing the necessary expertise for implementing the regulation."^^xsd:string .

:Article_68 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 68 — Scientific panel of independent experts"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_68_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_ii,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_iii,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_iv,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_3_v,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_68_5 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/68/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU is setting up a panel of independent experts to help enforce its AI regulations. The panel will be made up of experts chosen by the Commission, who have up-to-date knowledge in AI and are independent from any AI system providers. They will support the AI Office by alerting them to potential risks, helping develop evaluation tools, and advising on the classification of AI models. The experts will work impartially and confidentially, and will not take instructions from anyone. They will also have to declare any interests to avoid conflicts. The Commission will decide the number of experts needed and ensure fair representation."^^xsd:string .

:Article_78 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 78 — Confidentiality"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32019R1020 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_78_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_78_5 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/78/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU AI Act states that all parties involved in applying the regulation must respect the confidentiality of information and data they obtain. This includes protecting intellectual property rights and trade secrets. They can only request data necessary for assessing the risk of AI systems and must have cybersecurity measures in place to protect this information. The data must be deleted once it's no longer needed. Information exchanged confidentially cannot be disclosed without prior consultation. The Act doesn't affect the rights or obligations of the Commission, Member States, and their authorities to exchange information and issue warnings."^^xsd:string .

:Article_89 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 89 — Monitoring actions"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_89_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_89_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_89_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_89_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_89_2_c ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/89/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The AI Office has the power to monitor how well companies using general-purpose AI models are following the rules set out in this Regulation. This includes checking if they're sticking to approved codes of practice. Companies that use these AI models (downstream providers) can also complain if they think the rules have been broken. They need to provide details about who they're complaining about, what they think has gone wrong, which part of the Regulation has been breached, and why they believe this. They can also include any other relevant information they've found themselves."^^xsd:string .

:Article_95 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 95 — Codes of conduct for voluntary application of specific requirements"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_95_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_2_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_95_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/95/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU's AI Office and member states will encourage the creation of codes of conduct for AI systems. These codes will promote voluntary adherence to certain standards, taking into account technical solutions and industry best practices. They will also encourage the use of AI in a way that minimizes environmental impact, promotes AI literacy, ensures inclusivity and diversity, and prevents negative impacts on vulnerable groups. These codes can be created by AI providers, deployers, or their representative organizations, and should consider the interests and needs of small businesses and startups."^^xsd:string .

:Article_97 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 97 — Exercise of the delegation"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_97_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_97_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_97_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_97_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_97_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_97_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/97/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article explains how the European Commission can adopt delegated acts, which are essentially specific laws or regulations. The Commission has the power to do this for five years from when the regulation comes into effect, but this can be extended unless the European Parliament or Council objects. The Commission must consult with experts from each EU member state before adopting a delegated act. Once an act is adopted, it must be notified to the European Parliament and Council. The act only comes into force if neither the Parliament nor Council objects within three months."^^xsd:string .

