:Article_46 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 46 — Derogation from conformity assessment procedure"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_46_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_46_7 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/46/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article allows for exceptions to the usual approval process for high-risk AI systems. If there's a justified reason, such as public safety or environmental protection, a market surveillance authority can allow these systems to be used for a limited time while they're being assessed. In urgent situations, law enforcement or civil protection authorities can use these systems without prior approval, as long as they request it soon after. If approval is denied, the system must be stopped and its results discarded. The EU Commission and other member states must be informed of any approvals, and if no objections are raised within 15 days, the approval is considered justified. If objections are raised, the Commission will consult with the relevant parties and decide if the approval is justified. If it's not, the approval must be withdrawn."^^xsd:string .

:Article_26 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 26 — Obligations of deployers of high-risk AI systems"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32016R0679 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_26_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_10,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_11,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_12,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_26_9 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article outlines the responsibilities of those who deploy high-risk AI systems. These include using the systems as per instructions, assigning human oversight, ensuring input data is relevant, and monitoring the system's operation. If a risk is identified, the provider and relevant authorities must be informed immediately. Deployers must also keep logs generated by the AI system for at least six months. Before using a high-risk AI system, workers must be informed. If the system is not registered in the EU database, it should not be used. Deployers must also comply with data protection assessments and cooperate with relevant authorities."^^xsd:string .

:Legislation_32019R1020 a :EULegislation ;
    rdfs:label "EU Legislation 32019R1020",
        "EU legislation 32019R1020"@en ;
    :hasCELEXNumber "32019R1020",
        "32019R1020"^^xsd:string .

:Article_16 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 16 — Obligations of providers of high-risk AI systems"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_16,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_k,
        :Paragraph_Article_16_l ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/16/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that companies providing high-risk AI systems must follow certain rules. They must make sure their AI systems meet specific standards and display their contact information on the product or its packaging. They need to have a quality management system and keep certain documents and logs. Before selling or using the AI system, they must have it checked for compliance with regulations. They also need to mark the product with a CE marking to show it meets EU standards. They must register the product, fix any issues, provide necessary information, and prove it meets standards if asked by authorities. The AI system must also be accessible according to EU directives."^^xsd:string .

:Article_43 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 43 — Conformity assessment"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_43_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_43_6 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/43/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article discusses the process of assessing whether high-risk AI systems meet certain standards. If a provider has used certain standards in creating their AI system, they must choose one of two assessment procedures. If the provider hasn't used these standards, or only used part of them, they must follow a specific assessment procedure. If the AI system is for law enforcement or immigration authorities, a market surveillance authority will act as the assessment body. The article also states that if an AI system is significantly modified, it must undergo a new assessment. The Commission can update these procedures as technology advances."^^xsd:string .

:ThirdPartyAssessment_Annex_VII a eu-aiact:ConformityAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Annex VII third-party conformity assessment procedure"@en ;
    :hasAnnexReference :Annex_VII ;
    :hasArticleReference :Article_43 ;
    :hasRequiredComponent :conformity_step_annvii_1,
        :conformity_step_annvii_2,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_a,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_b,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_c,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_d,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_e,
        :conformity_step_annvii_3_f,
        :conformity_step_annvii_4,
        :conformity_step_annvii_4_a,
        :conformity_step_annvii_4_b,
        :conformity_step_annvii_4_c,
        :conformity_step_annvii_5 .

:Annex_VII a :Annex ;
    rdfs:label "Annex VII — Conformity based on an assessment of the quality management system and an assessment of the technical documentation"@en ;
    :hasSourceURL "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/7/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "Article 43 provides some scenarios in which a 'conformity assessment' might be required. This Annex outlines the process for assessing the quality management system and technical documentation of AI systems.In summary: The provider must submit an application including details about the AI systems, quality management procedures, and a declaration that the application hasn't been submitted elsewhere. The notified body will assess the system and notify the provider of the decision. Any changes to the system must be reported to the notified body. The provider must also submit an application for the assessment of the technical documentation of the AI system.The notified body will examine the documentation and may require further evidence or tests. If the AI system meets the requirements, a certificate will be issued. If not, the provider will be informed of the refusal and the reasons. Changes to the AI system that could affect compliance must be assessed by the notified body. The provider must allow the notified body to access the premises for assessment purposes and share all necessary information. The notified body will carry out periodic audits to ensure the provider maintains the quality management system."^^xsd:string .

:Article_9 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 9 — Risk management system"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_9_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_10,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_2_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_2_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_2_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_2_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_5_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_5_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_5_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_9_9 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/9/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU AI Act requires a risk management system for high-risk AI systems. This system should be a continuous process throughout the AI's lifecycle, regularly reviewed and updated. It should identify and analyze potential risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights, estimate and evaluate these risks, and adopt measures to manage them. These measures should balance minimizing risks and fulfilling requirements. The system should also ensure that any remaining risk is acceptable and that measures are in place to eliminate or reduce risks as much as possible. High-risk AI systems should be tested to identify the best risk management measures and to ensure they work as intended. The system should also consider whether the AI could negatively impact people under 18 or other vulnerable groups."^^xsd:string .

:Article_14 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 14 — Human oversight"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_14_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_3_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_3_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_4_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_14_5 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article states that high-risk AI systems must be designed in a way that allows humans to effectively oversee them. The goal of human oversight is to prevent or minimize risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights that may arise from using these systems. The oversight measures should match the risks and context of the AI system's use. These measures could be built into the system by the provider or implemented by the user. The AI system should be provided in a way that allows the overseer to understand its capabilities and limitations, detect and address issues, avoid over-reliance on the system, interpret its output, decide not to use it, or stop its operation. For certain high-risk AI systems, any action or decision based on the system's identification must be verified by at least two competent individuals."^^xsd:string .

:Article_17 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 17 — Quality management system"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_17_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_k,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_l,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_1_m,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_17_4 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "The EU AI Act requires providers of high-risk AI systems to establish a quality management system. This system must be documented and include strategies for regulatory compliance, design and development procedures, testing and validation processes, technical specifications, data management systems, risk management, post-market monitoring, incident reporting, communication procedures, record-keeping, resource management, and an accountability framework. The implementation of these aspects should be proportionate to the size of the provider's organization. Providers already subject to quality management obligations under relevant Union law can include these aspects in their existing systems. Financial institutions can meet these requirements by complying with Union financial services law."^^xsd:string .

:Article_60 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 60 — Testing of high-risk AI systems in real world conditions outside AI regulatory sandboxes"@en ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_60_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_2,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_3,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_c,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_d,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_e,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_f,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_g,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_h,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_i,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_j,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_4_k,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_5,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_6,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_7,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_8,
        :Paragraph_Article_60_9 ;
    :hasSourceURL "data/unstructured/eu_ai_act_content.html"^^xsd:string,
        "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/60/"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasSummary "This article discusses the rules for testing high-risk AI systems in real-world conditions. Companies that want to test these systems must submit a testing plan to the relevant authority in the EU member state where the testing will take place. If the authority doesn't respond within 30 days, the plan is considered approved. The testing can't last longer than six months, but it can be extended for another six months if necessary. The companies must also ensure that the testing doesn't harm vulnerable groups and that any data collected is properly protected. If any serious incidents occur during testing, they must be reported and the testing must be suspended until the issue is resolved."^^xsd:string .

:TechnicalDocumentation_Annex_IV a airo:Documentation,
        eu-aiact:TechnicalDocumentation ;
    rdfs:label "Annex IV technical documentation"@en ;
    :hasAnnexReference :Annex_IV ;
    :hasArticleReference :Article_11 ;
    :hasRequiredComponent :documentation_component_anniv,
        :documentation_component_anniv_a,
        :documentation_component_anniv_b,
        :documentation_component_anniv_c,
        :documentation_component_anniv_d,
        :documentation_component_anniv_e,
        :documentation_component_anniv_f,
        :documentation_component_anniv_g,
        :documentation_component_anniv_h ;
    :hasSummary "The technical documentation referred to in Article 11(1) shall contain at least the following information, as applicable to the relevant AI system: 1. A general description of the AI system including: (a) its intended purpose, the name of the provider and the version of the system reflecting its relation to previous versions; (b) how the AI system interacts with, or can be used to interact with, hardware or software, including with other AI systems, that are not part of the AI system itself, where applicable; (c) the versions of relevant software or firmware, and any requirements related to version updates; (d) the description of all the forms in which the AI system is placed on the market or put into service, such as software packages embedded into hardware, downloads, or APIs; (e) the description of the hardware on which the AI system is intended to run; (f) where the AI system is a component of products, photographs or illustrations showing external features, the marking and internal layout of those products; (g) a basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer; (h) instructions for use for the deployer, and a basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer, where applicable; 2. A detailed description of the elements of the AI system and of the process for its development, including: (a) the methods and steps performed for the development of the AI system, including, where relevant, recourse to pre-trained systems or tools provided by thi"^^xsd:string .

:Article_5 a :Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 5 — Prohibited AI practices"@en ;
    :cites :Legislation_32016R0679 ;
    :hasParagraphReference :Paragraph_Article_5_1,
        :Paragraph_Article_5_1_a,
        :Paragraph_Article_5_1_b,
        :Paragraph_Article_5_1_c,
